<!-- ============================ THE PATTERN ============================ -->
<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Reading the Chart</p>
    <h2 id="t-what-the-comparison-shows">What the Comparison Actually Shows</h2>
    <p><strong>It is a monism with a moral spine.</strong> On ultimate reality the Law of One sits closest to
    <em>Advaita Vedanta</em> &mdash; the non-dualism that says the self and the ground of being are one. But
    where Shankara's non-dualism dissolves the many into the One as illusion, Ra keeps the many as a real
    school, and bolts onto the monism a sharp ethical dualism &mdash; serve or seize &mdash; that Vedanta does
    not require.</p>
    <p><strong>It is a reincarnationism with a deadline.</strong> Like Hinduism and Buddhism it teaches rebirth
    and liberation. Unlike them it adds a quantitative threshold and a cosmic clock: a percentage, a harvest,
    a graduation. The wheel has an exit ramp with a posted speed limit. That is a modern, almost bureaucratic
    turn, and it is worth noticing as such.</p>
    <p><strong>Its dualism is the strange part.</strong> No major tradition so evenly blesses two opposed
    paths. Christianity, Islam, the Dharmic faiths all treat the selfish path as a failure, a fall, a fetter.
    Ra treats it as a legitimate, coherent, if ultimately self-defeating, way up. That single move &mdash;
    refusing to call the negative path <em>wrong</em>, only narrow &mdash; is the most distinctive thing in the
    material, and the one most worth arguing with.</p>
    <p><strong>And its authority is unlike all of them.</strong> Every classical canon grounds itself in
    history, prophecy, or a founder's awakening. The Law of One grounds itself in a contact &mdash; a voice,
    tranced, in a room, on tape. That is its vulnerability and, oddly, its honesty: it shows its work,
    publishes its doubts, and tells you to disbelieve it where it does not ring true.</p>
  </div>
</section>

